Q: How can I color pixels that are not black in bitmap in yellow using LockBits? A: <p>How about simply testing the bytes and setting them accordingly?</p>

<pre><code>Byte r1, g1, b1;
r1 = rgbValues1[counter];         // should be + 2 !!
b1 = rgbValues1[counter + 1];     // should be + 0 !!
g1 = rgbValues1[counter + 2];     // should be + 1 !!

if (r1 + b1 + g1 == 0 ) 
{
    r1 = 255;
    g1 = 255;
}
</code></pre>

<p>Of course this assumes a real black and a basic yellow are ok with you..</p>

<p>If you need more control, you need a bit more code like</p>

<pre><code>if (r1 + b1 + g1 &lt; threshold)
</code></pre>

<p>for shades of black, and for the yellows maybe: </p>

<pre><code>    r1 = myYellow_R;
    g1 = myYellow_G;
    b1 = myYellow_B;
</code></pre>

<p><strong>BTW</strong>: You need to check on those indices; the last time I looked the data in the LockBits array were <strong>reversed</strong>: not <code>RGB</code>, (let alone <code>RBG</code> as you have it) but <code>BGR</code>! (And for 32bpp <code>BGRA</code> !)</p>

<p>Since you are using that old method, please also make sure your pixel format is ok; if it is <code>32bpp</code> you'll need a few simple modifications.</p>

Q: How can I remove extra indentation from CodeBlockItemListSyntax? A: <pre><code>This is just a workaround and peice of the puzzle.

So whenever working with &quot;&quot;&quot;String Blocks&quot;&quot;&quot; ( maybe there is a more widely reconized name in the community, )
I've found that aligning the the entire block against the left-most margin can prevent certain errors/unexpected-behaviors I was having...

Example:

var test0: String =
&quot;&quot;&quot;
struct MyStruct0 {
  let id = UUID()
}
&quot;&quot;&quot;

instead of:

var test1: String = &quot;&quot;&quot;
  struct MyStruct1 {
    let id = UUID()
  }
&quot;&quot;&quot;

I found the same to be true while testing your Macro:

@main
struct Client {
  static func main () {
    #measure {
      doSomething()
      doSomethingElse()
    }
  }
}

produced: &quot;doSomething()\n.    doSomethingElse()&quot;
while:


@main
struct Client {
   static func main () {

#measure {
doSomething()
doSomethingElse()
}

   }
}

produced the expected result: &quot;doSomething()\ndoSomethingElse()&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>*/</p>

Q: Spring Boot Failed to execute goal org.apache.maven.plugins:maven-surefire-plugin:2.22.1:test A: <p>Updating the jar plugin to 3.1.1 immediately fixes the problem:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;properties&gt;
  &lt;maven-jar-plugin.version&gt;3.1.1&lt;/maven-jar-plugin.version&gt;
&lt;/properties&gt;
</code></pre>

<p><a href="https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/16846#issuecomment-492792506" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/16846#issuecomment-492792506</a></p>

Q: Error 500: javax.servlet.ServletException: Filter [SBTFilter] A: <p>There is a sample project called social.helloworld.webapp. You can look at the following web.xml 
<a href="https://github.com/OpenNTF/SocialSDK/blob/master/samples/j2ee/templates/social.helloworld.webapp/src/main/webapp/WEB-INF/web.xml" rel="nofollow">https://github.com/OpenNTF/SocialSDK/blob/master/samples/j2ee/templates/social.helloworld.webapp/src/main/webapp/WEB-INF/web.xml</a></p>

<p>Copy the Following Lines</p>

<pre><code>&lt;servlet&gt;
&lt;description&gt;The service servlet handles requests from the toolkit to access external resources.&lt;/description&gt;
&lt;display-name&gt;Social Business Toolkit Service Servlet&lt;/display-name&gt;
&lt;servlet-name&gt;ServiceServlet&lt;/servlet-name&gt;
&lt;servlet-class&gt;com.ibm.sbt.service.core.servlet.ServiceServlet&lt;/servlet-class&gt;
&lt;/servlet&gt;
&lt;servlet-mapping&gt;
&lt;servlet-name&gt;ServiceServlet&lt;/servlet-name&gt;
&lt;url-pattern&gt;/service/*&lt;/url-pattern&gt;
&lt;/servlet-mapping&gt;
&lt;servlet&gt;
&lt;description&gt;This servlet initializes the specified JavaScript library for use by the Social Business Toolkit.&lt;/description&gt;
&lt;display-name&gt;Social Business Toolkit Library Servlet&lt;/display-name&gt;
&lt;servlet-name&gt;LibraryServlet&lt;/servlet-name&gt;
&lt;servlet-class&gt;com.ibm.sbt.jslibrary.servlet.LibraryServlet&lt;/servlet-class&gt;
&lt;/servlet&gt;
&lt;servlet-mapping&gt;
&lt;servlet-name&gt;LibraryServlet&lt;/servlet-name&gt;
&lt;url-pattern&gt;/library/*&lt;/url-pattern&gt;
&lt;/servlet-mapping&gt;
&lt;filter&gt;
&lt;description&gt;This filter is responsible for creating the toolkit application and context objects for every servlet within this web application.&lt;/description&gt;
&lt;display-name&gt;Social Business Toolkit Filter&lt;/display-name&gt;
&lt;filter-name&gt;SBTFilter&lt;/filter-name&gt;
&lt;filter-class&gt;com.ibm.sbt.util.SBTFilter&lt;/filter-class&gt;
&lt;/filter&gt;
&lt;filter-mapping&gt;
&lt;filter-name&gt;SBTFilter&lt;/filter-name&gt;
&lt;url-pattern&gt;/*&lt;/url-pattern&gt;
&lt;/filter-mapping&gt;
&lt;resource-ref&gt;
&lt;description&gt;Reference to a URL resource which points to the managed bean configuration for the Social Business Toolkit.&lt;/description&gt;
&lt;res-ref-name&gt;url/ibmsbt-managedbeansxml&lt;/res-ref-name&gt;
&lt;res-type&gt;java.net.URL&lt;/res-type&gt;
&lt;res-auth&gt;Container&lt;/res-auth&gt;
&lt;res-sharing-scope&gt;Shareable&lt;/res-sharing-scope&gt;
&lt;/resource-ref&gt;
&lt;resource-ref&gt;
&lt;description&gt;Reference to a URL resource which points to the configuration properties for the Social Business Toolkit.&lt;/description&gt;
&lt;res-ref-name&gt;url/ibmsbt-sbtproperties&lt;/res-ref-name&gt;
&lt;res-type&gt;java.net.URL&lt;/res-type&gt;
&lt;res-auth&gt;Container&lt;/res-auth&gt;
&lt;res-sharing-scope&gt;Shareable&lt;/res-sharing-scope&gt;
&lt;/resource-ref&gt; 
</code></pre>

<p>and then restart your webapp
you can also make sure that the jar files exist. </p>

<p>Another good reference is... <a href="https://github.com/OpenNTF/SocialSDK/wiki/Building-your-first-social-enabled-jsp" rel="nofollow">https://github.com/OpenNTF/SocialSDK/wiki/Building-your-first-social-enabled-jsp</a></p>
